1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,373
Three friends. A tiny shed in New Zealand. Three months of work.

2
00:00:04,723 --> 00:00:08,473
And then, fourteen million people playing their game at the same time.

3
00:00:08,823 --> 00:00:17,122
This is the story of 99 Nights in the Forest, one of the biggest games Roblox has ever seen. And the formula behind it, which you can actually learn from.

4
00:00:17,472 --> 00:00:27,360
The game was made by a studio called Grandma's Favourite Games. Three guys from Auckland. Two have been friends since school, and they met the third one on Roblox as teenagers.

5
00:00:27,710 --> 00:00:40,719
In an interview with PC Games N, they said they started in a shed with just their desks in it. They started building in March twenty twenty-five, and released in June. Their earlier games took around six months. This time, speed was the goal.

6
00:00:41,069 --> 00:00:51,029
Then things got crazy. In September twenty twenty-five, the game averaged about one hundred thirty-seven million visits per day, more than Grow a Garden, the previous summer hit.

7
00:00:51,379 --> 00:00:58,517
By late twenty twenty-six, it passed twenty-nine billion visits. It got added to Fortnite, and a movie is in the works.

8
00:00:58,867 --> 00:01:09,577
How much money did it make? Roblox doesn't publish that. But revenue trackers estimate around two hundred forty million dollars over its lifetime. Treat that as a rough estimate, not a fact.

9
00:01:09,927 --> 00:01:14,570
So why did it work? I studied the game, and I found a formula with six parts.

10
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:23,411
One. The promise is in the name. Ninety nine nights, in the forest. A number, a place, a goal. You understand the whole game in one second.

11
00:01:23,761 --> 00:01:32,147
Two. A simple loop. During the day, you gather and build. At night, you defend. Anyone can learn it in a minute, even on a phone.

12
00:01:32,497 --> 00:01:40,874
Three. One object at the center of everything: the campfire. Upgrade the fire, and the map gets bigger. You literally see your progress.

13
00:01:41,224 --> 00:01:50,944
Four. A monster you cannot kill. The Deer. You can only stun it with a flashlight. That makes it iconic. Memes, fan art, merch, even a movie.

14
00:01:51,294 --> 00:01:56,803
Five. A reason to care. You are not just surviving. You are trying to rescue four missing kids.

15
00:01:57,153 --> 00:02:13,028
And six. Friends, and timing. Up to five players together, which creates panic, screaming, and funny moments, perfect for TikTok and YouTube. Weekly updates while it was growing. And a release right at the start of summer, when Roblox is busiest.

16
00:02:13,378 --> 00:02:21,455
But here is the part people miss. You cannot copy their luck, and you cannot copy their years of experience. What you can copy is the formula.

17
00:02:21,805 --> 00:02:30,644
Their own advice was simple. Know your product better than anyone. Trust your instincts. And don't be afraid to fail. Try things, and move on if they don't work.

18
00:02:30,994 --> 00:02:45,826
And that's exactly what I'm doing. I'm nineteen, from Kyrgyzstan, and I'm building Roblox games using this formula. My main game is called 33 Days. Your plane crashes in the Amazon, and search teams stop looking for you in thirty-three days.

19
00:02:46,176 --> 00:02:52,432
If you want to watch me build it, and see the real numbers when it launches, subscribe. See you in the next one.
